{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3bd","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b3be-22c5-72c2-9a68-fcdf0b06f553"},"sequence":57,"recorded_at":1780531400000109,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"4c090c0f-8ac7-469e-b4bd-d568cdc91e07","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"4c090c0f-8ac7-469e-b4bd-d568cdc91e07","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"4c090c0f-8ac7-469e-b4bd-d568cdc91e07"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0b3be-49fa-70c1-9ba3-c1873aeb24c4"},"task_id":"01a0b3be-49fa-70c1-9ba3-c1873aeb24c4","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0b3be-49fa-70c1-9ba3-c1873aeb24c4","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-2056mm/TASK-260830-2056mm_producer.md`.\n1|Own TASK-260830-2056mm (implement-terminal-binding-events-and-recovery), the FINAL leaf of STORY-260830-ptxkqe (ax-pane-and-terminal-instance-binding, EPIC M1). Predecessors TASK-260830-1geqhj (the `ax pane` enforcement wrapper decision core and its durable bootstrap binding) and TASK-260830-kkh1an (the Section 4.C Terminal Instance lifecycle contract over internal/terminalbackend) are accepted and checkpointed on the Story branch. Your Change Request is the story_final whole-Story patch, so read the whole branch delta (`git -C .temp/STORY-260830-ptxkqe/worktree log --stat <trunk>..HEAD`) and both predecessors' outcome resources before you start. Note that 1geqhj's revision 1 was rejected for authoring `session.parked` under a lease the local host does not hold and for never composing `fencing.AuthorizeMutation`; revision 2 fixes the authoring path — build on the CORRECTED path, and if you find it still authors under a lease this host does not hold, stop and say so in the results instead of adding a second writer.\n2|Scope, against pinned Sections 5.2 (Session Event 4.0.0 Terminal Instance events), 4.B, 4.D, 7.A and 13.1:\n3|(1) The two v4 payloads, exactly: `terminal.created` = terminal_binding_id:digest, terminal_backend_id:terminal-backend-id, implementation_version:semver, protocol_version:semver, evidence_ids:sorted unique digest[1..256]; `session.resumed` = checkpoint_id:digest, execution_profile:standard|yolo, profile_source_event_id:digest|null, terminal_binding_id:digest, terminal_backend_id:terminal-backend-id, implementation_version:semver, protocol_version:semver, evidence_ids:sorted unique digest[1..256]. Session Event 4.0.0 retains the exact v3 top-level closed shape, digest rules, lease ordering, predecessor rules and the complete v1-v3 registry — every other payload stays byte-for-byte the v3 definition. Author both through the landed `sessrepo` append path under a lease the local host holds, with the closed-shape discipline of the landed canonicaljson; a missing member, an extra member, an unsorted or duplicated evidence_ids array and both bounds (1 and 256, plus 0 and 257) must each refuse with the pinned class.\n4|(2) Evidence resolution. `evidence_ids` MUST resolve locally to validated, sanitized Manifest, Probe and applicable Capability Evidence objects bound to the EVENT's backend ID and versions — drive that through the landed internal/terminalbackend admission, and prove the negative directly: an evidence ID that resolves to a native reference, a generation string, a socket, a pipe, an endpoint, a token, a credential, terminal output, a PID/handle or any live-process fact must refuse. The binding digest is an opaque audit reference: the Terminal Instance Binding object itself is not an event member and is never replicated (ship a test that fails if a binding object, native_reference or generation string can be reached through the event).\n5|(3) Identity refusal (4.B, 7.A): `terminal_instance_id` is an AX-allocated UUIDv7 and identity MUST NOT be a PID, handle, socket, path, named pipe, URL, token or mutable endpoint — in the Binding, the Provider Protocol 3 descriptor, the operation bodies and the CLI Result 4 summary alike. Drive each of those eight forbidden forms as an input and pin the refusal; a package-wide census of what the code persists is not enough on its own, so pair it with the driven refusals.\n6|(4) Lost-result recovery by bootstrap operation ID (13.1, 4.1): after a lost `create` result, `status` plus the durable (session_id, bootstrap_operation_id) binding MUST prove absence or identify the ONE child — never a second child, never a claim of `absent` when the effect cannot be disproven (that case is `unavailable` with `status_first`, per the lifecycle contract kkh1an landed). Drive a real lost result (kill between effect and result), a duplicate retry with the same operation, and a retry with a changed operation inside and outside the bootstrap window.\n7|(5) Version skew and authority: a valid takeover or owner resume MAY select a different admitted destination backend and emits a NEW binding digest and a new v4 event — it never mutates an earlier event, never clones or forks the Logical Session; attach or presentation-client activity emits NEITHER event and cannot change Owner/Replica, lease or fencing state (drive an attach and assert zero events and zero state change); a v1-v3 reader retains a v4 event as inert immutable history and MUST NOT derive runtime state from it or write a lower-version event (drive both directions through the landed sessstate/sessquery readers).\n8|Tests and evidence: every clause driven through a production entry with a named committed test, an n-of-m ratio and call sites; negatives for every refusal; crash/idempotency evidence at the event-append and binding seams; a narrowing mutant per gate (drop an evidence-resolution check, widen an array bound by one, let an attach emit an event, let a v4 payload member through at v3, drop the lease-authority check) executed by a shipped harness with per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits and an applied harmless SURVIVED control; and a conformance matrix over every 5.2/4.B/4.D/7.A/13.1 clause this Story claims, mapped to a test, a stated bound or NOT APPLICABLE with its owner.\n9|COMMON CONTRACT (FINAL leaf of its Story — your Change Request is the story_final whole-Story patch). Normative authority: the pinned internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md headings named above; the v0.7.0 adoption (STORY-260917-110onn) may land on trunk during your run — if it has landed when you refresh, cite SPEC.v0.7.0.md with the same heading numbers and use the v0.7.0 artifacts everywhere the v0.6.0 ones are named below. Reuse the landed owners (canonicaljson closed shapes, scalar, sessrepo/sessstate/sessquery, sessckpt, matjournal, crashgate, fencing, sessprofile, provhost/resumesmoke, hosttrust/peeridentity/sshtransport, rpcwire/hostchannel, sessadapter, terminalbackend, localstore, secprim/secconftest) and above all the packages your own Story's earlier leaves added on this branch — never fork a second model of anything already modeled; extend or adopt and say which. STORY-CLOSE ITEMS, all owned by this leaf: (a) internal/traceability ownership registry bindings for every normative section this Story claims plus the Story's acceptance case bound to the REAL conformance suite with honest coverage text that separates executed lanes from stated bounds (precedent Stories 3drr2m/3jqsx1/3m2mw8/2jylym/1kiyj6) — edit the ADOPTED registry, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.7.0.json` if the v0.7.0 adoption is on trunk when you refresh, otherwise `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.6.0.json`, and never invent a binding for a clause no test drives; (b) re-derive and re-pin `reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256` in internal/traceability/traceability.go from the semantic JSON projection of the registry you just edited — derive it, never copy a digest from another Story, and show the derivation command and output in the results; (c) `go run ./internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck` green plus the section-scoped run for each section you bound, with the measured coverage ratio quoted verbatim in the results; (d) README: a section for each package this Story added with its exact test commands and NO capability or CLI claim (no `ax` command exists), and the \"Measured coverage of this repository\" subsection updated to the numbers tracecheck actually prints; (e) one newest-first LOGBOOK entry for the Story. The candidate's task-board.config.json must stay byte-identical to trunk unless you deliberately add a bounded validation command — if you add one, say so explicitly in the results and keep it fast. Evidence contract (repository DoD): every AC row driven through a production entry by a named committed test with a stated n-of-m ratio and call sites; negatives for every refusal; a narrowing mutant per gate executed by a shipped harness with per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits and an applied harmless SURVIVED control (PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, no __pycache__); crash/idempotency evidence for every durable write or an explicit purity bound; a conformance matrix mapping each pinned clause to a test, a stated bound or NOT APPLICABLE with an owner. Managed Story worktree contract: the Story worktree .temp/STORY-260830-ptxkqe/worktree on branch task-board/story/STORY-260830-ptxkqe ALREADY EXISTS and its tip is the previous leaf's signed checkpoint commit — do not re-provision, rebase, commit on, amend or revert it, never edit .task-board directly, never self-accept. Your candidate is the UNCOMMITTED working tree on top of that checkpoint; the CR the runtime builds from it is the WHOLE-Story patch, so read every earlier leaf's delta (`git -C .temp/STORY-260830-ptxkqe/worktree log --stat <trunk>..HEAD`) and its outcome resources before you start. If origin/main moves before your handoff run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-2056mm` (LOGBOOK append conflicts are resolved with --replay-resolutions: copy the retained resolution-template.json header and add {\"checkpoint_oid\": <the ORIGINAL checkpoint OID recorded before the refresh>, \"path\": \"LOGBOOK.md\", \"sha256\": <hex of the full replacement>, \"content\": <base64>} keeping every entry newest-first), then audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old-base> <new-base>` that also appears in your `git status` and restore trunk content by hand — a refresh carries stale pre-refresh copies of trunk-changed files into the candidate and they read as reverts (README/LOGBOOK purely additive; task-board.config.json == HEAD; the ownership registry must MERGE the trunk rows with yours, never replace them). Before handoff `git status` shows only candidate paths. Read the Curator-managed go-testing-tools skill first; all CI local; run the full configured validation suite (30 commands as of trunk c3aae73 — STORY-260830-4qojoz added three bounded rpcwire fuzz gates; read the count from the candidate config rather than assuming it) once before handoff (command 5 is `go test ./... -race -count=1 -timeout 25m` and can take 25-40 minutes under concurrent runs — that is expected, do not shorten it; command 21 is `cataloggen -adopted … -check`). Hand off no later than 3.5 hours in — if the full scope cannot be finished, hand off a reviewable slice with the remaining items enumerated precisely in the results. Attach TASK-260830-2056mm_results.md, TASK-260830-2056mm_conformance-matrix.md and TASK-260830-2056mm_producer-evidence.tar.gz (a real gzip archive), keep the checklist current, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-2056mm --role developer` and exit promptly. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be claude-opus-5 max.\n10|\n11|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n12|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n13|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n14|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n15|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n16|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n17|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n18|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.\n19|\n20|TRUNK STATE AT YOUR SPAWN (recorded 2026-09-18): origin/main is c3aae73 as of 2026-09-18T01:30Z and carries BOTH the v0.7.0 adoption (PR #52, commit 2fc6d50) and the mesh-RPC Story plus the resumesmoke deadline fix (PR #53 a12d1bd, PR #54 c3aae73) — internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md, internal/specpin/v0.7.0.lock.json, internal/catalog/catalog.v0.7.0.json with `catalog.Adopted = ReleaseV070`, internal/traceability/ownership.v0.7.0.json, and traceability.go pointing at that trio. Cite SPEC.v0.7.0.md with the same heading numbers as the v0.6.0 sections named above. If origin/main moves again before your handoff, run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate <this task>` and then audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old base> <new base>` that also appears in your `git status`: README.md and LOGBOOK.md moved on trunk with that landing, so keep your edits purely additive on top of the NEW content and never restore the old bytes; task-board.config.json must end byte-identical to the new base.\n21|\n22|LANDED FACT YOU MUST NOT GET WRONG (found by the independent review of TASK-260830-1geqhj rev2, and it cost that leaf a whole round): `Observation.Winner.HasCheckpoint` / `Winner.Checkpoint` is a LEASE RECORD field, and lease records are immutable content-addressed blobs. `sessrepo.CreateLease` mints the epoch-1 lease with a NULL checkpoint, and only `CompareAndSwapLease` — a takeover, epoch >= 2 — mints a lease that carries one (Section 5.3: \"Null only for epoch-1 create; otherwise the validated materialized handoff base\"). No number of published checkpoints ever puts one on the epoch-1 lease. So any gate keyed on that field is VACUOUS for every session that never changed hands, which is the ordinary same-host story. The landed fact for \"the newest checkpoint\" is the chain fold: `sessstate.Reduce` -> `Projection.HasCheckpoint` / `Newest.ID`, derived from `checkpoint.created`, checkpointed `session.stopped` and `session.resumed` (Section 5.7 `newest_checkpoint_id`, Section 14.4). Key any window, admission or binding rule on that fold, and where a successor lease also carries a checkpoint, ASSERT the two agree rather than letting one silently win.","final_result":true}}}
